==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Latinized translation of {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|Bohème}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|Boiohaemum}}, compound of Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*haimaz||home}} (more at {{l|en|home}}) and ''[[w:Boii|Boio]]''- ‘the Boii’, the {{etyl|cel|en}} tribe previously inhabiting the area. The endonym is from {{etyl|cel-pro|en}} {{m|cel-pro|*boio}} and could ultimately be from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*gʷṓws}}, a reference to cattle owners, or from {{m|ine-pro|*bhei-||warrior, strong hitter}}.

Bohemia was abandoned by the Boii ''ca''. 60 BCE. and settled by the Germanic [[w:Marcomanni|Marcomanni]] shortly thereafter.<ref>Dietz, Karlheinz (Würzburg). “Boiohaemum.” ''Brill’s New Pauly'', 2012. Reference. 14 March 2012 [http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/brill-s-new-pauly/boiohaemum-e218860]</ref> Related to {{l|en|Bavaria}}.

